{"schema_version": 1, "run_id": "run-20260831-233055", "scored_iteration": 11, "action": "draft", "parent_node_id": null, "direction": "watchtime", "node_id": "n0010", "hypothesis": "Train a single factorization machine to regress the signed log-watchtime margin relative to an empirically learned duration-dependent long-view threshold, rather than treating every positive or negative as equally informative. Dense distance-to-threshold supervision should improve estimates for sparse users and items while retaining the exact long-view sign.", "rationale": "The baseline already has healthy within-user score dispersion, so additional categorical capacity or calibration is unlikely to address the main error. Play time provides substantially denser information than a binary long-view label: an impression barely below the long-view boundary and one abandoned immediately should not impose identical supervision. Unlike the prior shared multitask attempt, this model has one aligned objective: robust regression of a signed watchtime margin, with play_time_ms used only to construct train targets. The duration-dependent boundary is learned exclusively from train by finding the best playback threshold within duration strata. A heteroskedastic AFT-style score, dividing predicted margin by train-estimated duration-bucket dispersion, is also evaluated per epoch because the probability of crossing zero depends on both expected margin and variance.", "references": ["Factorization Machines (Rendle, 2010)", "Accelerated failure time models (Buckley and James, 1979)", "Robust estimation with Huber loss (Huber, 1964)"], "changes": ["Replace pointwise long-view BCE with Huber regression on a signed log(play_time)-to-threshold margin.", "Learn the duration-dependent long-view playback threshold from train labels and train play times in 32 duration strata.", "Use long_view only to enforce the sign of the dense watchtime target when a coarse duration stratum does not perfectly recover the boun